{
  "concept": "To define the line integral of a scalar density f along a space curve, the curve from P to Q is chopped into n small arcs; the mass of the kth arc is approximated by the density at any sample point on that arc times the arc length, ΔM_k ≈ f(x*_k, y*_k, z*_k)Δs_k, and summing these gives a Riemann sum that converges to ∫_C f ds.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Main smooth blue curve with a loop, running from lower-left endpoint P = P_0 up through a self-intersecting loop to upper endpoint Q = P_n",
    "Blue filled circular partition dots along the curve at P_0, P_1, P_2, P_3, ..., P_{k-1}, P_k, ..., P_{n-1}, P_n",
    "Labels: P = P_0 (lower left), P_1, P_2, P_3 (lower arm), P_{k-1} and P_k (on the loop, with leader line to P_k), P_{n-1}, Q = P_n (top)",
    "Arc-length caliper annotation on the main curve: two short tick bars at P_{k-1} and P_k with a double-headed curved arrow labeled Δs_k",
    "Leader arrow from the text 'Mass ΔM_k' pointing to the highlighted kth arc on the loop",
    "Highlighted kth arc segment of the main curve (thicker / accented stroke) between P_{k-1} and P_k",
    "Large tapered pale-orange zoom arrow with grey outline sweeping from the kth arc down-right to the inset",
    "Zoom inset at lower right: isolated blue arc from P_{k-1} (left blue dot) to P_k (right blue dot)",
    "Inset caliper: two tick bars at the arc ends with double-headed arrow labeled Δs_k",
    "Red sample dot on the inset arc labeled P*_k(x*_k, y*_k, z*_k)",
    "Grey-bordered formula box at the bottom: ΔM_k ≈ f(x*_k, y*_k, z*_k) Δs_k",
    "Readout panel (added for the demo): current n, Riemann sum Σ f(P*_k)Δs_k, and the limiting value ∫_C f ds, with the running error",
    "Faint density shading/legend along the curve indicating where f is large or small"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "numSegments",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Number of subdivisions n",
      "range": [3, 60, 1],
      "default": 8,
      "teaches": "Refining the partition makes each Δs_k shrink and the sum of the arc masses Σ f(P*_k)Δs_k settle onto a single number — the line integral ∫_C f ds."
    },
    {
      "id": "selectArc",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Any one of the n arcs can play the role of the 'kth' piece; clicking an arc on the main curve re-labels it P_{k-1}→P_k and loads it into the zoom inset, showing the construction is identical everywhere along the curve."
    },
    {
      "id": "dragSamplePoint",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "The red sample point P*_k may sit anywhere inside the arc: moving it changes f(x*_k, y*_k, z*_k) and hence the estimate of ΔM_k, but as Δs_k shrinks every choice gives the same limit — which is why the definition does not depend on the sample points."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A curve with mass",
      "narration": "A thin wire follows the curve C from P = P_0 to Q = P_n, with variable density f(x, y, z) shown by the shading along it. We want its total mass.",
      "state": { "numSegments": 3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Chop the curve into arcs",
      "narration": "Partition points P_0, P_1, ..., P_n cut C into n small arcs. The kth arc has length Δs_k and carries an unknown mass ΔM_k.",
      "state": { "numSegments": 8 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Zoom in on one arc",
      "narration": "Click any arc to isolate it. On such a short piece the density is nearly constant, so mass ≈ density × length: ΔM_k ≈ f(x*_k, y*_k, z*_k) Δs_k.",
      "state": { "numSegments": 8 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Does the sample point matter?",
      "narration": "Drag the red point P*_k anywhere inside the arc. The estimate wobbles a little now — but the wobble is bounded by how much f varies across a single arc.",
      "state": { "numSegments": 8 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Refine the partition",
      "narration": "Push n up. Each Δs_k shrinks, the sample-point wobble becomes invisible, and the Riemann sum Σ f(P*_k)Δs_k locks onto the true line integral ∫_C f ds.",
      "state": { "numSegments": 45 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "On a short enough arc the density is essentially constant, so mass ≈ f(sample point) × arc length; summing these local products and letting the arcs shrink defines ∫_C f ds — and in the limit it no longer matters which point in each arc you sampled.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two-panel layout on a white background. Left/upper two-thirds: a single smooth bright-blue curve (stroke ~4px) that rises from a lower-left endpoint, sweeps up-right, then curls into a closed-looking loop in the upper-left before continuing up to the top endpoint — it crosses itself once. Blue filled dots mark the partition points, spaced roughly evenly in arc length, with serif-italic math labels P = P_0 (bottom left), P_1, P_2, P_3 up the lower arm, P_{k-1} and P_k on the lower edge of the loop, P_{n-1} near the top, Q = P_n at the top. A small caliper (two short vertical tick bars joined by a curved double-headed arrow) sits above the kth arc labeled Δs_k; a thin curved leader from the text 'Mass ΔM_k' points up to the same arc. A large pale-orange tapered zoom arrow with a grey outline sweeps from the kth arc down and right. Lower right: the isolated kth arc drawn large as a gentle blue upward curve with blue endpoint dots labeled P_{k-1} (left) and P_k (right), its own Δs_k caliper above, and a red dot on the arc labeled P*_k(x*_k, y*_k, z*_k). Bottom center: a grey rectangular box containing ΔM_k ≈ f(x*_k, y*_k, z*_k) Δs_k. For the demo add a small unobtrusive readout in a corner giving n, the Riemann sum, and the exact integral; the selected arc is drawn thicker and in a warmer accent color while the other arcs stay standard blue.",
  "figureCaption": "Figure 15.2.2 — Partitioning a space curve to define the line integral of a scalar function."
}

