  "reconstructionNotes": "Two-panel layout on a white background. Left/upper two-thirds: a single smooth bright-blue curve (stroke ~4px) that rises from a lower-left endpoint, sweeps up-right, then curls into a closed-looking loop in the upper-left before continuing up to the top endpoint — it crosses itself once. Blue filled dots mark the partition points, spaced roughly evenly in arc length, with serif-italic math labels P = P_0 (bottom left), P_1, P_2, P_3 up the lower arm, P_{k-1} and P_k on the lower edge of the loop, P_{n-1} near the top, Q = P_n at the top. A small caliper (two short vertical tick bars joined by a curved double-headed arrow) sits above the kth arc labeled Δs_k; a thin curved leader from the text 'Mass ΔM_k' points up to the same arc. A large pale-orange tapered zoom arrow with a grey outline sweeps from the kth arc down and right. Lower right: the isolated kth arc drawn large as a gentle blue upward curve with blue endpoint dots labeled P_{k-1} (left) and P_k (right), its own Δs_k caliper above, and a red dot on the arc labeled P*_k(x*_k, y*_k, z*_k). Bottom center: a grey rectangular box containing ΔM_k ≈ f(x*_k, y*_k, z*_k) Δs_k. For the demo add a small unobtrusive readout in a corner giving n, the Riemann sum, and the exact integral; the selected arc is drawn thicker and in a warmer accent color while the other arcs stay standard blue.",
  "figureCaption": "Figure 15.2.2 — Partitioning a space curve to define the line integral of a scalar function."
}
